// JSON, read and written, for sysl.
//
// A document reads into an ordinary sysl value that owns itself -- an array is a `Buf[Json]` and an
// object a `Buf[Member]`, with no reference anywhere -- so every walk over one is a plain `match`.
// It reads as well as writes, because the two halves are the same package's job: a program that
// changes one member of a configuration file and writes it back needs both.
//
// This is the block a consumer writes to reach it:
//
//     dependencies {
//       json { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/json", version = "0.1.1" }
//     }
//
// The coordinate names the package, `json`; the module a program imports is `sh.sysl.json`. Naming
// the coordinate alone is enough to import `sh.sysl.parsing` too, since imports are transitive --
// which a caller needs, because `parse` takes a `Source` and answers with a `Diagnostic`.

package {
  name    = "json"
  version = "0.1.1"

  // **The oldest compiler this builds with, and the reason is one declaration.** `Obj(members:
  // Buf[Member])` over a named `struct Member` holding a `Json` is a cycle that passes through
  // `Buf`'s `[]T`, and every compiler before this one refused it -- *"type 'Json' contains itself,
  // so it has no finite size"* -- against a reference page saying the cycle is legal. Card `0268`
  // fixed it, and until it did this package's central type could not be spelled.
  //
  // **That is what the package is for as much as the reading is**: a named member with a `key` and
  // a `value` is what a caller walking an object reads, and the shapes an older compiler does
  // accept -- a tuple with no field names, or two buffers a caller keeps in step -- are public
  // payloads that could not be walked back later without breaking every consumer.
  sysl = "0.0.79"
}

dependencies {
  parsing { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/parsing", version = "0.2.0" }
}

// **`heap = true`, and every one of the three reasons is the same one.** A document's size is not
// known until it has been read: an array is a `Buf[Json]`, an object a `Buf[Member]`, and a string
// with an escape in it is gathered into a builder. Nothing here can be given caller-supplied
// storage, because nothing here knows how much to ask for.
//
// **What is NOT here is `os` or `posix`.** This package neither opens nor reads anything: a `Source`
// is made over text the caller already has, and rendering goes into a sink the caller passes. Where
// the document came from and where it is going are the program's business.
requires {
  heap = true
}
